!deck: Math::Extra
!tags: Math Calculus Center-of-Mass
!type: Basic Math

!id: MoLv0577jW
!front: Define the first mass moment \( \mathbf{M}_O \in \mathbb{R}^d \) of a finite system of particles about the origin.
!back:
For particles with masses \( m_i > 0 \) and position vectors \( \mathbf{r}_i \in \mathbb{R}^d \) measured from the origin, the first mass moment about the origin is

\[ \mathbf{M}_O = \sum_{i=1}^n m_i \mathbf{r}_i. \]
~~~

!id: 0RcMegQfoW
!front: Define the center of mass \( \mathbf{R} \in \mathbb{R}^d \) of a finite system of particles.
!back:
For particles with masses \( m_i > 0 \) and position vectors \( \mathbf{r}_i \in \mathbb{R}^d \) measured from a common origin, the center of mass is

\[ \mathbf{R} = \frac{1}{m} \sum_{i=1}^n m_i \mathbf{r}_i = \frac{\mathbf{M}_O}{m}, \]

where \( m \) is the total mass

\[ m = \sum_{i=1}^n m_i > 0. \]
~~~

!id: ZCB3iIT1h5
!front: Define the first mass moment \( \mathbf{M}_O \in \mathbb{R}^d \) of a bounded continuous body about the origin.
!back:
Let \( D \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d \) be a bounded region with integrable mass density \( \rho:D \to [0,\infty) \). The first mass moment about the origin is

\[ \mathbf{M}_O = \int_D \rho(\mathbf{r})\mathbf{r}\,dV. \]
~~~

!id: 4mowVIcLBq
!front: Define the center of mass \( \mathbf{R} \in \mathbb{R}^d \) of a bounded continuous body.
!back:
Let \( D \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d \) be a bounded region with integrable mass density \( \rho:D \to [0,\infty) \) and positive total mass. The center of mass is

\[ \mathbf{R} = \frac{1}{m}\int_D \rho(\mathbf{r})\mathbf{r}\,dV = \frac{\mathbf{M}_O}{m}, \]

where \( m \) is the total mass

\[ m = \int_D \rho(\mathbf{r})\,dV > 0. \]
~~~

!id: 291eW9ie3t
!front: How are the discrete and continuous center-of-mass formulas related?
!back:
They have the same weighted-average structure:

\[
\begin{align*}
  \mathbf{R}_{\text{discrete}} &= \frac{\sum_{i=1}^n m_i\mathbf{r}_i}{\sum_{i=1}^n m_i}, \\
  \mathbf{R}_{\text{continuous}} &= \frac{\int_D \rho(\mathbf{r})\mathbf{r}\,dV}{\int_D \rho(\mathbf{r})\,dV}.
\end{align*}
\]

The continuous formula replaces individual masses \( m_i \) at positions \( \mathbf{r}_i \) by infinitesimal mass elements \( \rho(\mathbf{r})\,dV \) distributed over \( D \).
~~~
